# j1abs — problems and the fix that worked

Same J1a ISA, image port `j1a`, no kernel of its own, no `con_core` row. The word script still has to run on this body. bcpu's own kernel failures are `.cursor/rules/bcpu-console.mdc`. Do not give j1abs that queue or that row.

## The baremetal blink fires once

The first program stored 1, then 0, then spun. Reset re-executes address 0: `reboot` forces the next PC to 0 after the first instruction, and the stack effect still happens. `firmware/blink_j1abs.4th` is a loop. The `on` label is the first literal, so the extra push happens only at reset. `drop` after `io!` drops the address. Nested countdowns are about a tenth of a second at 50 MHz.

## A typed line is not the script that exports the gap

The hand launch is `fsoc --build` in `projects/soc_j1abs` with no `FSOC_EMU_UART_GAP`. The panel stores keystrokes on the host. UART does not see them, so a slow finger is not a slow wire. Enter calls `push_line`, the same call as `FSOC_EMU_UART_IN`, and the bytes leave back to back unless `gap_clocks` is set. A script that exports 120 still passes when `uart.gap` is missing, and that is the miss: the agent checked the script, the typed line came out corrupt, Forth answered `?`, and the session was stopped with Ctrl+C (`Emulation stopped`).

`sim.sh` reads `uart.gap` (120 from the j1abs profile) only when the variable is unset. Setting it, including to 0, wins. The check is `env -u FSOC_EMU_UART_GAP` and `FSOC_EMU_UART_IN='1 2 + .'` → `3  ok`. Do not turn the gap on for j1a or bcpu.

J1 UART RX holds one byte and arms on a falling edge. One `emit` on the 51-clock core already runs past the next start bit. `accept` echoes BS as three `emit`s when the byte 8 is inside the UART stream (a script, not the panel: the panel eats BS locally). Without the gap that line never becomes `13 .`. j1a stays inside the start bit, so the same script passes there.

## The word session dies at 2e9 cycles

`atoi` into a signed int stops at 2^31−1. The j1a script on this core needs more. `FSOC_EMU_CYCLES` is `strtoull`. The full script is `gforth tests/j1abs_words.4th` (24e9 cycles, oracle cpu `j1a`, dictionary `fsys-j1a.json`). It is not named `*_test.4th`: fmix prints nothing until a file returns, and this run is several minutes. It is still the last step before review, together with baremetal blink and soc blink at `FSOC_EMU_EDGES=4` (both pin levels twice). Spec: `openspec/specs/forth-cross/spec.md`. `tests/j1abs_test.4th` only sends `1 2 + .` and the backspace line. Grep `3  ok`, not `3` (`313 words` is in the build log).

## Verilator 4 and 5 in one obj_dir

The user shell is `/usr/bin/verilator` 4.038. oss-cad-suite is 5.053 and is often first on the agent PATH. A leftover `verilated.o` is newer than the 2020 sources, so make does not rebuild it, and the link fails (`timeunit`, `VlThreadPool`). `fsoc --clean`, then one Verilator for that build. Tests use a fresh temp dir. Yosys is the oss-cad-suite binary; do not pass `-q` or `stat` is hidden. The gate is `3 DP16KD`.

## Host strings and the image port

`fsoc-path+` frees its input. `2dup` then `fsoc-path+` is a use-after-free and the copy prefix doubles. The `rom_init.vh` check uses `fsoc-path`, then frees only the absolute copy. `fasm-pc` does not exist when `cg-i-blink.4th` loads; resolve it with `find-name` at run time. Hardware log is `cpu-ref(id)` → `j1(j1abs)`. Gforth 0.7.3 has no `name>interpret`; the builder needs 0.7.9.

## Already true — do not undo

- `code_addr` stays on the current instruction until the commit cycle. The wrap loads `insn` on that edge.
- `alu_q` and `ctrl_q` are a separate `always` with no reset, or the tables do not map to block RAM.
- Do not compile this `j1.v` in the same iverilog run as j1a `j1.v` (`WIDTH` is global). Pass `-I cpu/j1/j1abs`.
- Stack RAM init is `55aa`, one bit per address, low bit first. The initial assignment has to be a 1-bit value (`word[0]`). `(16'h55aa >> (i & 15)) & 1` is a width warning, and Verilator 5 exits on it. Do not change the pattern to match a word-test underflow.
- A command is 51 clocks (steps 0..50). `tests/tb` counts `cpu.commit`, not a phase code.
- One 512×16 field (23 clocks, packed ALU, word-wide stack writes) routed to 347 LUT4 and 205 DFF. That is more cells than this core. Do not put it back.
- `soc.4th` has no `j1abs` literal. `con_core_test.4th` has no `j1abs` row.
